b48-c0dd-5ca6-a760-7383939e9b88" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163008 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163009 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163009 #edb/ref 1061 34623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163009 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb7de1b2-0c12-5011-8752-ebd8e223e8d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163009 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1061 38977 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6c5298f8-ee76-510b-9852-47db3df79bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163111 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1061 39007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f84ee295-698e-570e-8b1d-f95c9890f4ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1061 39008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d02007b7-e508-588a-8b76-1a1887b8fb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163114 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1061 39009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "54ee6fbf-8ad8-5c2d-b013-e251b532c2d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163115 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186163343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186163009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1061 39010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2be6a036-867e-5d15-93fd-01acf1d032ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163116 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1064 "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance. \n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big].\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y,$ and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0.\n$$\n\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(X-\\text{E}[X])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{(X-\\text{E}[X])}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0\n$$\n\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2.$ This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called  **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1065 5624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "aa0d2bd6-235b-5d66-abc0-b8869af0ae0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163290 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm  E\\Big[\\left(X− \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y−\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big].\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]]-\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y]+\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1065 5635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "57fd5059-dc1e-5473-9f12-db4e68dc88af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163292 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Covariance Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1064 "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/c5/c589b62f579cb5efe7c4a2b86aa9bebbbbec81517c8bd7872c3c6a0b019e08cc.svg)\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x+\\frac{y}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1065 5637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "066864ea-8aaa-5094-bf78-7c862b1dd64e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163293 #edb/ref 1067 "Covariance for Continuous Random Variables" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1064 "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?  \n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y].\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1065 6455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8bb09eee-27a4-5c3e-b10d-923dbaa105df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186163343 #edb/ref 1067 "A Formula for Quickly Computing Covariance" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526495 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526495 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526495 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186526495 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186527259 #edb/ref 17