186527094 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527094 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527094 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c0679843-7790-5fe7-98d3-601893fb4657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147969" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29a09d79-668a-5d76-93b7-d90b94b6b6a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527144 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.6$ | $0$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.85$ | $0.15$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+1.2 \\\\  & =1.2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.85+1\\cdot 0.15 \\\\  & =0+0.15 \\\\  & =0.15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.2)(y-0.15)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.2)(0-0.15)f(0,0) \\\\  & +(0-1.2)(1-0.15)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(0-0.15)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(1-0.15)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-1.2)(-0.15)(0.25) \\\\  & +(-1.2)(0.85)(0.15) \\\\  & +(0.8)(-0.15)(0.6) \\\\  & +(0.8)(0.85)(0) \\\\  & =-0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1071 "If $\\text{E}[X]=5.5,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4.38,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=24.12$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7980335d-9e66-542b-a520-d910d32ed247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527171 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =24.12-(5.5)\\cdot (4.38) \\\\  & =0.03.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y$ is $0.03.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527023 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146386" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0.8$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=2.2,$ calculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aa0cadd2-5104-5faa-b07c-30440395ac28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527195 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.1)+(0)(2)(0.2)+(0)(3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(2)(1)(0.1)+(2)(2)(0.2)+(2)(3)(0.1) \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.6-(0.8)(2.2) \\\\  & =-0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1071 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculate the covariance between $X$ and $Y.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b5d80cd0-76ca-5260-b15c-cf7c6e9faf1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527202 #edb/ref 1074 "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=[0,2]\\times [0,4].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $h(y)=\\frac{y}{8}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147965" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1071 "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.11$ | $0.44$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.09$ | $0.36$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e6e3c2bf-143f-5ac7-a0ed-5faf8562286b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527222 #edb/ref 1074 "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527230 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527230 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "09565298-9c43-54e4-9ad2-16dc8176d4eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527230 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527233 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527233 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "12270953-49ad-5cbc-9697-5a803cf8a2d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527233 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527249 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4f7e1c6e-7d68-545c-b26a-04a011d3cdbd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527249 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527259 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527259 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "710840c6-390b-5ef5-bf9d-aa99fdfbc7aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527259 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527284 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527284 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ed3e6a7c-c1b0-5aee-98e7-a9f3c2d96919" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527284 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527222 #edb/ref 175921860